// `com.microsoft.GroupQueryAttention` — fused GQA with RoPE (serial per invocation).
//
// # Correctness before performance
//
// One GLSL invocation per (batch, query_head, query_seq_pos).  There are no barriers, no shared
// memory dependencies and no wave-level reductions — the correctness surface is a plain serial
// algorithm, and it stays that way at every workgroup size.  The dispatch is
// `(ceil(B * Nq * S / local_size), 1, 1)`.
//
// # The workgroup size is a specialisation constant, and why that is a performance fix
//
// This kernel shipped with `local_size = (1,1,1)`.  A workgroup of one invocation does not
// occupy one lane of the machine — it occupies a whole SIMD group with one lane enabled, because
// the hardware's unit of scheduling is the subgroup, not the invocation.  On the RTX A1000 the
// subgroup is 32 wide, so **31 of every 32 lanes were masked off for the entire kernel**.
//
// MEASURED 2026-08-06 (`bench/results/real_model_gqa_local_size.json` ->
// `timing[].points[local_size == 1].by_kernel_us`, key `vulkan.gpu.gqa_f16` against that point's
// `total_us`; Foundry Phi-3.5 int4 on an RTX A1000): `gqa_f16` was **64.6%** of all GPU time at
// prefill `M = 128` (1891.19 of 2927.34 ms) and **93.5%** at decode with a 1024-token cache
// (268.57 of 287.18 ms) — the largest single consumer of device time in the model, ahead of the
// quantised GEMM this EP has spent its optimisation effort on.  `real_model_diagnostics.json` is
// **not** the source and carries no per-kernel GPU field at all — only ORT's node table, counters,
// fallback and dispatch records.  An earlier revision of this comment cited it, and quoted 64.7%;
// both are withdrawn.  `docs/DESIGN.md` §8.13 and `docs/PERF.md` §26.4 print the same rows from
// the same field.
//
// Nothing about the arithmetic changes: invocation `gid` computes exactly what it computed
// before, in the same order, reading and writing the same addresses.  Only the *packing* of
// invocations into workgroups changes, so the result is bit-identical by construction.  The
// atomics an older comment described as "never actually contended at size 1" were never really
// uncontended either: contention is a function of `seq_len`, not of the workgroup size, and they
// were always written for the contended case.  Packing does not add an ordering guarantee to
// anything, which is why step 3's half-word writes stay atomic and why the sibling-key branch in
// step 4 recomputes from a read-only input rather than reading a sibling's store back.
//
// The size is a specialisation constant rather than a literal because the right value is a
// function of how many invocations there are: a decode step has `B * Nq * S = 32` of them, and
// packing 32 invocations into one workgroup hands the whole kernel to a single compute unit
// while 32 workgroups of one invocation are spread across all of them.  `ops::attention::
// gqa_local_size` picks it, and `ONNXRUNTIME_EP_VULKAN_GQA_LOCAL_SIZE` overrides it.
//
// Invocations past the end of the grid are guarded: `b >= pc.batch_size` returns, and that is
// exactly the condition an over-dispatched tail satisfies (see `main`).
//
// # Why serial first
//
// Island attribution (2026-07-30): GroupQueryAttention is the sole op creating inter-island cuts
// in Phi-3.5's graph (32 nodes → 32 ORT topological breaks → 33 islands).  The 33-island
// profile shows command-buffer recording at 68.3% of wall time; reducing to ~1 island saves
// most of that overhead.  A correct serial kernel gives the reduction immediately; per-element
// tiling is a follow-up once this baseline is validated on both devices.
//
// # Scope (first kernel)
//
//   Supported:
//     - Packed QKV:   input_0 = [B, S, (Nq + 2*Nkv) * D]; inputs 1 and 2 absent
//     - KV cache:     past_key/value [B, Nkv, past_stride, D], present_key/value
//                     [B, Nkv, present_len, D].  Both the shared-buffer convention
//                     (present_len == past_stride, updated in place) and the growing
//                     convention (present_len == past_stride + S, past copied in) are
//                     supported; the two strides are separate push constants because they
//                     are only equal in the first case.
//     - RoPE:         half-rotation (neox) with do_rotary=1, rotary_interleaved=0
//     - Attention:    online softmax over [0 .. tok_pos] tokens, causal
//     - Output:       attn_output [B, S, Nq*D]; present_key/value written at tok_pos
//
//   Not supported by this kernel (all declined by claim predicate):
//     - Separate Q/K/V inputs
//     - rotary_interleaved=1
//     - Softcap, attention_bias, QK norm
//
// # Push-constant layout (must match translate_gqa in attention.rs)
//
//   offset  0: batch_size   u32
//   offset  4: seq_len      u32
//   offset  8: num_heads    u32    (Nq)
//   offset 12: kv_num_heads u32    (Nkv)
//   offset 16: head_dim     u32    (D)
//   offset 20: rotary_dim   u32    (rot_D; rot_D/2 cos/sin entries per position)
//   offset 24: present_len  u32    (present KV buffer S dimension = KV write stride)
//   offset 28: past_stride  u32    (past KV buffer S dimension)
//   offset 32: scale        f32    (1/sqrt(D))
//
// # Binding table (must match translate_gqa order)
//
//   binding 0: packed_qkv   readonly   [B, S, (Nq+2*Nkv)*D]   f16 packed as uint
//   binding 1: past_key     readonly   [B, Nkv, past_stride, D]   f16 packed as uint
//   binding 2: past_value   readonly   [B, Nkv, past_stride, D]   f16 packed as uint
//   binding 3: seqlens_k    readonly   [B]   i32  (actual past length per batch element)
//   binding 4: cos_cache    readonly   [max_seq, rot_D/2]   f16 packed as uint
//   binding 5: sin_cache    readonly   [max_seq, rot_D/2]   f16 packed as uint
//   binding 6: attn_output  writeonly  [B, S, Nq*D]          f16 packed as uint
//   binding 7: present_key  readwrite  [B, Nkv, present_len, D]   f16 packed as uint
//   binding 8: present_value readwrite [B, Nkv, present_len, D]   f16 packed as uint
//
// # f16 packing convention (shared with all other shaders in this project)
//
//   Tensors are stored as uint arrays, two f16 elements per uint.  Element at flat index i is
//   in word i/2; the lower 16 bits hold element i&~1, the upper 16 bits hold element i|1.
//   Reads use unpackHalf2x16; writes use atomicAnd/atomicOr to avoid corrupting the neighbour.
//   The atomics are what make any workgroup size correct: two invocations of the same workgroup
//   can hold the two halves of one 32-bit word, and a plain store would lose one of them.

#version 450
#extension GL_EXT_shader_16bit_storage : require

// Specialisation id 0 — the host's `gqa_local_size`. Defaults to 1 so a pipeline built without
// the constant behaves exactly as this kernel did before the constant existed.
layout(local_size_x_id = 0, local_size_x = 1, local_size_y = 1, local_size_z = 1) in;

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Push constants
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
layout(push_constant) uniform PC {
    uint  batch_size;
    uint  seq_len;
    uint  num_heads;
    uint  kv_num_heads;
    uint  head_dim;
    uint  rotary_dim;
    // KV *write* stride — the present buffer's S dimension. Under the growing-cache
    // convention this is past_stride + seq_len; under the shared-buffer convention the two
    // are equal. They were a single field while the shared case was assumed.
    uint  present_len;
    uint  past_stride;   // the past buffer's S dimension
    float scale;
} pc;

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Buffers (f16 elements packed two-per-uint)
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
layout(set = 0, binding = 0, std430) readonly  buffer BufQKV  { uint d[]; } qkv;
layout(set = 0, binding = 1, std430) readonly  buffer BufPK   { uint d[]; } past_k;
layout(set = 0, binding = 2, std430) readonly  buffer BufPV   { uint d[]; } past_v;
layout(set = 0, binding = 3, std430) readonly  buffer BufSeq  { int  d[]; } seqlens;
layout(set = 0, binding = 4, std430) readonly  buffer BufCos  { uint d[]; } cos_buf;
layout(set = 0, binding = 5, std430) readonly  buffer BufSin  { uint d[]; } sin_buf;
// attn_out is NOT declared writeonly: wr_out uses atomicAnd/atomicOr which need read access.
// Each invocation writes to a disjoint range (different (b,h,s_local)), so the atomics never
// contend for the same *element* — but two invocations of one workgroup can share a 32-bit
// word, which is precisely what the read-modify-write atomics are for.
layout(set = 0, binding = 6, std430)           buffer BufOut  { uint d[]; } attn_out;
layout(set = 0, binding = 7, std430)           buffer BufPresK { uint d[]; } pres_k;
layout(set = 0, binding = 8, std430)           buffer BufPresV { uint d[]; } pres_v;

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scalar f16 read helpers (one per readonly buffer — GLSL cannot parameterise
// buffer blocks, so each buffer gets its own helper)
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
float f16_qkv(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(qkv.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_pk(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(past_k.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_pv(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(past_v.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_cos(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(cos_buf.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
float f16_sin(uint i) {
    vec2 p = unpackHalf2x16(sin_buf.d[i >> 1u]);
    return (i & 1u) == 0u ? p.x : p.y;
}
// present_key and present_value are written but never read. The read helpers that used to
// exist here were removed with the sibling-key race: `present` is the one buffer in this
// shader that other invocations of the same dispatch are concurrently writing, so any read
// of it is unordered. Keeping a reader for it is keeping the race one edit away.

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scalar f16 write helpers (atomic to protect the neighbour half-word)
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
void wr_out(uint i, float v) {
    uint bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, v));
    if ((i & 1u) == 0u) {
        atomicAnd(attn_out.d[i >> 1u], 0xFFFF0000u);
        atomicOr (attn_out.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0x0000FFFFu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(attn_out.d[i >> 1u], 0x0000FFFFu);
        atomicOr (attn_out.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0xFFFF0000u);
    }
}
void wr_presk(uint i, float v) {
    uint bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, v));
    if ((i & 1u) == 0u) {
        atomicAnd(pres_k.d[i >> 1u], 0xFFFF0000u);
        atomicOr (pres_k.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0x0000FFFFu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(pres_k.d[i >> 1u], 0x0000FFFFu);
        atomicOr (pres_k.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0xFFFF0000u);
    }
}
void wr_presv(uint i, float v) {
    uint bits = packHalf2x16(vec2(v, v));
    if ((i & 1u) == 0u) {
        atomicAnd(pres_v.d[i >> 1u], 0xFFFF0000u);
        atomicOr (pres_v.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0x0000FFFFu);
    } else {
        atomicAnd(pres_v.d[i >> 1u], 0x0000FFFFu);
        atomicOr (pres_v.d[i >> 1u], bits & 0xFFFF0000u);
    }
}

// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Main
// --------------------------------------------------------------------------
void main() {
    uint gid = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;

    uint S   = pc.seq_len;
    uint Nq  = pc.num_heads;
    uint Nkv = pc.kv_num_heads;
    uint D   = pc.head_dim;
    uint rh  = pc.rotary_dim >> 1u;   // number of cos/sin entries per position = rotary_dim/2

    // Decode gid → (batch b, query head h, query seq offset s_local)
    uint b       = gid / (Nq * S);
    uint rem     = gid % (Nq * S);
    uint h       = rem / S;
    uint s_local = rem % S;
    // FAIL CLOSED, and the tail guard. `gid` runs to `local_size * ceil(total / local_size)`,
    // which exceeds `B * Nq * S` whenever the workgroup size does not divide it. Every such
    // invocation lands at `b >= batch_size` by construction — `gid >= B * Nq * S` implies
    // `gid / (Nq * S) >= B` — so this one comparison is the whole bounds check, and it was
    // already here when the grid was exact.
    if (b >= pc.batch_size) return;

    uint kv_h    = h / (Nq / Nkv);     // KV head (group_size = Nq/Nkv; MHA has group_size=1)
    // ORT defines `seqlens_k[b]` as total_sequence_length - 1, and total = past + seq_len.
    // So the past extent is `seqlens_k[b] + 1 - seq_len`, NOT `seqlens_k[b]`.
    //
    // Those two expressions are equal exactly when seq_len == 1, which is decode, which is
    // every case this kernel had. At prefill the old reading placed the first query token
    // at absolute position `past + seq_len - 1` instead of `past`, so it indexed past_key
    // beyond its extent and wrote present beyond its own. Another decode-only assumption
    // that a decode-only test set cannot see -- the same shape as the `present == past`
    // and `rotary_dim == head_dim` defaults.
    int  seqlens_b = seqlens.d[int(b)];
    uint past_len  = uint(max(seqlens_b + 1 - int(S), 0));
    uint tok_pos   = past_len + s_local; // absolute position of this query token

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 1. Load Q, K, V from packed_qkv
    //    packed_qkv layout: [B, S, (Nq + 2*Nkv) * D]
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    uint qkv_row = (b * S + s_local) * (Nq + 2u * Nkv) * D;
    uint q_base  = qkv_row + h             * D;
    uint k_base  = qkv_row + Nq            * D + kv_h * D;
    uint v_base  = qkv_row + (Nq + Nkv)   * D + kv_h * D;

    // Local arrays — head_dim ≤ 128 for all current target models
    float q[128], k_new[128], v_new[128], acc[128];
    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
        q[d]     = f16_qkv(q_base + d);
        k_new[d] = f16_qkv(k_base + d);
        v_new[d] = f16_qkv(v_base + d);
        acc[d]   = 0.0;
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 2. Apply RoPE to Q and K (neox / half-rotation: first rh dims are "x", next rh are "y")
    //    cos_cache[pos, ri] is the ri-th cos value at sequence position pos.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
        uint cos_row = tok_pos * rh;
        for (uint ri = 0u; ri < rh; ri++) {
            float c = f16_cos(cos_row + ri);
            float s = f16_sin(cos_row + ri);

            float qx = q[ri];        float qy = q[ri + rh];
            q[ri]      = qx * c - qy * s;
            q[ri + rh] = qy * c + qx * s;

            float kx = k_new[ri];    float ky = k_new[ri + rh];
            k_new[ri]      = kx * c - ky * s;
            k_new[ri + rh] = ky * c + kx * s;
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3. Write rotated K/V into present_key/value at position tok_pos.
    //    present layout: [B, Nkv, present_len, D]
    //    (Atomic writes — half-word stores into a `uint` array, so two invocations writing the
    //    two halves of one word must not lose each other's write.  Contention is a function of
    //    the *data*, not of the workgroup size: sibling invocations on the same kv_head co-exist
    //    whenever seq_len > 1, in the same workgroup or in different ones.  The workgroup size is
    //    a specialisation constant chosen by the host and is not knowable here, so nothing in
    //    this file may assume it is 1.)
    //
    //    Capacity guard. Under the growing convention present_len is past_stride + seq_len and
    //    tok_pos < present_len always. Under the shared-buffer (arena) convention present_len
    //    is a *capacity* the caller chose, and tok_pos is derived from seqlens_k, so a caller
    //    who runs past the arena would index outside the buffer. An out-of-bounds store in
    //    GLSL is undefined — on this project it has already meant a dropped write that read
    //    back as zero, and it can also mean a device loss. Bound it. A skipped write is not
    //    silently accepted: it changes `present`, and every arena lane carries a bit-exact
    //    correctness control against the shipping lane that reads before any byte count.
    //
    //    Group leader. `present` is indexed by `kv_h = h / G`, not by `h`, so all G query heads
    //    of one KV group name the *same* words here. They wrote identical values — `k_new` and
    //    `v_new` are read from `k_base`/`v_base`, which are functions of (b, s_local, kv_h)
    //    only, and the RoPE applied to them uses `tok_pos`, which is a function of (b, s_local)
    //    only; `h` does not appear in either — so the redundancy was invisible to every
    //    correctness test and cost G x the KV write bandwidth. On Phi-3.5 G is 1 and there is
    //    nothing to see; on a 4-way grouped model this was 4 writes of the same half-word.
    //
    //    Exactly one invocation per (b, kv_h, s_local) has `h % G == 0` — namely `h = kv_h * G`
    //    — and it maps back to this same `kv_h`, so the single writer covers every word the G
    //    used to and no word loses a writer. This is the same predicate `copy_leader` below
    //    already uses for the past-region copy, and for the same reason.
    //
    //    Note what this changes about the masks. The G writers were what made the masked
    //    half-word writes *safe by redundancy*: complementary `And`/`Or` masks and identical
    //    values meant any interleaving ended correct. With one writer that argument is gone,
    //    and the safety now rests on disjointness instead: the surviving writer owns both
    //    halves of every word it touches, because the base `(b*Nkv + kv_h)*present_len*D +
    //    tok_pos*D` and the extent `D` are both multiples of 2 whenever D is even, and D is
    //    even for every model this kernel claims. The atomics are kept for the odd-D case,
    //    where two *different* (kv_h, tok_pos) rows can share a word — that sharing is between
    //    different leaders, so it survives the deduplication and still needs the mask.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    uint group_size = Nq / Nkv;
    bool kv_write_leader = (h % group_size) == 0u;
    if (kv_write_leader && tok_pos < pc.present_len) {
        uint pb = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.present_len * D + tok_pos * D;
        for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
            wr_presk(pb + d, k_new[d]);
            wr_presv(pb + d, v_new[d]);
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 3a. Growing cache: the past tokens must appear in `present`.
    //     ORT's present_key/value output is the WHOLE cache, past ++ new. Under the
    //     shared-buffer convention present IS past and the tokens are already there; under
    //     the growing convention present is a separate, LARGER allocation and nothing else
    //     puts the past in it.
    //
    //     This used to be a standalone loop here that re-read the entire past cache purely
    //     to relocate it. That read was pure waste: step 4 below already reads every one of
    //     those same elements, for the same (b, kv_h), in the same invocation whenever this
    //     invocation is the copy leader. Two reads of the same bytes, one dispatch apart.
    //
    //     The copy is now FUSED into step 4: the leader writes each past element to
    //     `present` at the moment it loads it for the attention sum. Loads drop from 2x the
    //     cache to 1x; the writes are unchanged, because ORT's contract requires the past
    //     region of `present` to be materialised and no arrangement of ours avoids that.
    //     Per-token KV traffic goes from (Nq/Nkv + 2) x cache to (Nq/Nkv + 1) x cache.
    //
    //     Note what this does NOT do: it does not remove the write. Under the growing
    //     convention nothing can: ORT's `present` is a separate, strictly larger allocation and
    //     its past region must be materialised.
    //
    //     What *does* remove it is the caller changing convention, not the kernel changing
    //     arithmetic. Under the shared-buffer (arena) convention `present_len == past_stride`,
    //     `copy_leader` is false for every invocation, and this whole fused copy compiles out
    //     of the run: the past tokens are already at the addresses `present` names, because
    //     `present` and `past` are the same allocation. That is the third lever on the KV
    //     ledger and it is a caller-side declaration (`ONNXRUNTIME_EP_VULKAN_KV_ARENA`), not a
    //     shader mode.
    //
    //     Aliasing is sound here for a reason that is checkable rather than hoped for: with one
    //     common stride, this invocation reads only `t < past_len` and writes only
    //     `tok_pos = past_len + s_local >= past_len`, at the same base. The read set and the
    //     write set of the entire dispatch are disjoint. The one read of `present` that ever
    //     existed — the sibling key at `past_len <= t < tok_pos` — was removed on 2026-08-02
    //     and recomputed from `packed_qkv`; had it survived, the arena would have turned a race
    //     into a corruption.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    bool copy_leader =
        pc.present_len != pc.past_stride && s_local == 0u && kv_write_leader;
    // The leader's own attention loop runs t over [0, past_len] inclusive, so it covers
    // every past position exactly once. Any other s_local would too, but only s_local == 0
    // is guaranteed to, and one writer per (b, kv_h) is what keeps the writes unduplicated.
    uint copy_dst = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.present_len * D;

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 4. Online softmax attention: softmax(Q K^T * scale) V, causal mask.
    //
    //    Online softmax (Milakov & Gimelshein, 2018) accumulates the weighted V sum in a
    //    single pass without storing the full score vector.  This eliminates the need for
    //    a MAX_TOTAL_SEQ-sized local array and keeps register pressure manageable.
    //
    //    Invariant: acc[d] == sum_{t=0}^{cur} exp(s_t - max_s) * V[t][d]
    //               sum_e  == sum_{t=0}^{cur} exp(s_t - max_s)
    //    Rescaling: when a new maximum max_s' > max_s is found, multiply acc and sum_e by
    //               exp(max_s - max_s') before adding the new term.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    float max_s = -1.0e30;
    float sum_e = 0.0;

    uint total_len = tok_pos + 1u;   // causal: [0 .. tok_pos] inclusive

    for (uint t = 0u; t < total_len; t++) {
        // Compute Q . K[t]
        float dot = 0.0;
        uint kv_base;
        if (t < past_len) {
            kv_base = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.past_stride * D + t * D;
            if (copy_leader) {
                // Fused step 3a: the element is loaded once and used twice.
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
                    float kv = f16_pk(kv_base + d);
                    dot += q[d] * kv;
                    wr_presk(copy_dst + t * D + d, kv);
                }
            } else {
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) dot += q[d] * f16_pk(kv_base + d);
            }
        } else if (t == tok_pos) {
            // self-key — use rotated k_new directly
            for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) dot += q[d] * k_new[d];
        } else {
            // t in [past_len, tok_pos): a key belonging to a *sibling query position in this
            // same dispatch*.
            //
            // This used to read it back out of `present_key`, where a sibling invocation had
            // written it in step 3. Nothing orders those two invocations. Two cases, and
            // **neither** is fixed by the workgroup size:
            //
            //   * The sibling is in another workgroup. Vulkan gives no execution or memory
            //     ordering between workgroups of one dispatch, full stop.
            //   * The sibling is in *this* workgroup. Packing invocations together buys
            //     nothing here either: there is no barrier on this path, the write went to a
            //     storage buffer rather than to shared memory, and invocations within a
            //     workgroup progress independently between barriers. A workgroup is not a
            //     lockstep unit and may not be treated as one.
            //
            // So the read was a data race on every prefill at every size — `seq_len > 1` is
            // exactly the regime this branch exists for, and exactly the regime KV traffic is
            // large enough to be worth optimising in. It was invisible because decode
            // (`seq_len == 1`) never reaches this branch at all, so every test we have passes
            // without it.
            //
            // The value is recomputed from `packed_qkv` instead. That buffer is a read-only
            // dispatch input, so there is no cross-invocation dependency to order and no
            // barrier to get wrong — the recompute is what makes this branch correct at *any*
            // workgroup size, which is precisely why it is still necessary now that the size is
            // a specialisation constant. It is the *same* arithmetic step 2 applies — the same
            // cos/sin row, the same neox pairing — so the result is bit-identical to what the
            // sibling wrote, not an approximation of it. The cost is arithmetic, in a loop
            // that is already memory-bound.
            uint ko = (b * S + (t - past_len)) * (Nq + 2u * Nkv) * D + Nq * D + kv_h * D;
            uint cr = t * rh;
            for (uint ri = 0u; ri < rh; ri++) {
                float c  = f16_cos(cr + ri);
                float sn = f16_sin(cr + ri);
                float kx = f16_qkv(ko + ri);
                float ky = f16_qkv(ko + ri + rh);
                dot += q[ri] * (kx * c - ky * sn) + q[ri + rh] * (ky * c + kx * sn);
            }
            // Dimensions at or beyond rotary_dim are not rotated (partial rotary).
            for (uint d = 2u * rh; d < D; d++) dot += q[d] * f16_qkv(ko + d);
        }
        float sc = dot * pc.scale;

        // Online softmax rescale
        if (sc > max_s) {
            float factor = exp(max_s - sc);
            for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] *= factor;
            sum_e *= factor;
            max_s = sc;
        }
        float e = exp(sc - max_s);
        sum_e += e;

        // Accumulate e * V[t]
        if (t < past_len) {
            kv_base = (b * Nkv + kv_h) * pc.past_stride * D + t * D;
            if (copy_leader) {
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) {
                    float vv = f16_pv(kv_base + d);
                    acc[d] += e * vv;
                    wr_presv(copy_dst + t * D + d, vv);
                }
            } else {
                for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] += e * f16_pv(kv_base + d);
            }
        } else if (t == tok_pos) {
            for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] += e * v_new[d];
        } else {
            // Same race, same fix. V carries no rotary, so this is a straight read of the
            // sibling position's slice of `packed_qkv`.
            uint vo = (b * S + (t - past_len)) * (Nq + 2u * Nkv) * D
                    + (Nq + Nkv) * D + kv_h * D;
            for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] += e * f16_qkv(vo + d);
        }
    }

    // Normalise
    float inv_s = 1.0 / sum_e;
    for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) acc[d] *= inv_s;

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5. Write attn_output[b, s_local, h*D .. (h+1)*D - 1]
    //    output layout: [B, S, Nq * D]
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------
    {
        uint ob = (b * S + s_local) * Nq * D + h * D;
        for (uint d = 0u; d < D; d++) wr_out(ob + d, acc[d]);
    }
}

